  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain leftover from Hurricane Isaias continues \nthrough tonight before moving offshore Monday followed by a \nquick warm up in temperatures.\n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias continue to spread into \neastern MA this afternoon as the center of the low strengthens \nand passes north of the 40/70 benchmark later tonight. Starting \nto see some heavier rain bands setting up across southern CT and\nRI, slowly pushing north and east into MA, and see some \nrainfall rates of up to 0.5 inch per hour could be possible at \ntimes. Expecting moderate to heavy rain through midnight before \nlightening up in the early morning hours Monday, then finally \npushing offshore late Monday morning or early afternoon. \nWidespread rainfall totals of 0.75\"-1\" is expected, as high as \n1.5\" along the immediate south coast.\n\nSkies will clear quickly behind the low thanks to some mid-\nlevel dry air moving in from the north, allowing for a quick \nwarm-up tomorrow afternoon. Monday's high temperatures rise into\nthe upper 60s, possibly the low 70s in the CT River Valley.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few rounds of showers this week, first later \nTuesday into Tuesday night, then around Friday into Saturday. \n\nOur region should then get clipped by a pair of low pressures, \neach taking a different track. The first should pass from the \nGreat Lakes across northern New England Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight. The risk for showers arrives across northern MA during \nthe morning, then gradually moves south Tuesday night as the \nassociated cold front approaches. Drying out Wednesday as this \nfront finally moves farther offshore during the day. Expecting \nWednesday to have above normal temperatures prior to this cold \nfront moving by.\n\nHigh pressure over eastern Canada should briefly take over our \nweather Wednesday night into Thursday night. Temperatures \nexpected to trend below normal for Thursday.\n\nThe forecast for next Friday and Saturday still has timing \nuncertainties. That said, the latest guidance suite started to \nconverge toward a common track and timing solution. The most \nlikely period for rainfall still looks to be between noon Friday\nand midnight Saturday. Some questions to how quickly it dries \nout on Saturday, but most of the guidance suite is more \nprogressing than the GFS. Favored the consensus solution in this\nregard.\n\nTurning noticeably cooler this weekend, especially Saturday \nnight into Sunday.",
